@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Egress policy — read-allow / write-deny / DLP at the firewall MITM hop

component "internal/egresspolicy\npure decisions — Decide(req)" as CORE <<app>>
component "internal/egressproxy\nmartian RequestModifier chain" as PROXY <<app>>
component "internal/broker\ncredential inject / strip" as BROKER <<app>>

component "egress-policy-components.puml\nhost → proveo-egress → Squid;\nbroker then policy; destinations" as D1 <<app>>
component "egress-policy-layers.puml\nA method · B exfil sinks\nC DLP + budget · config" as D2 <<app>>
component "egress-policy-decide.puml\nprovider skip · allow path\nblock + 403" as D3 <<app>>

PROXY ARROW_MAIN BROKER : 1. credentials
PROXY ARROW_MAIN CORE : 2. policy
CORE ARROW_OPTIONAL D1
CORE ARROW_OPTIONAL D2
CORE ARROW_OPTIONAL D3

note right of CORE
  **Order matters.** The broker runs first so a request
  to the pinned provider already carries its real auth
  header when policy evaluates it; policy then decides
  method / destination / payload for everything else.

  Full layers A/B/C exist only in **firewall** mode —
  ""proxy"" and ""broker"" cannot decrypt TLS, so there is
  no body to scan and no header to strip.
end note

note bottom of D2
  Design bias: **allow inbound reads broadly, constrain
  outbound tightly** — a data-diode posture. Reading is
  ""GET"", small out, large in; exfiltration is data
  //out//: a POST body, a fat query string, a stream to
  an attacker sink. Destination allowlists are the wrong
  axis; the asymmetry is.

  Non-goal: a hermetic seal. The goal is turning
  "trivially exfiltrate a 9 KB .env" into "a few bytes,
  slowly, off-list, on the record".
end note

note bottom of D3
  Related: credential inject/strip on this same hop is
  specified in _spec/_paradigms/credential-boundary.puml;
  the claudecode topology in
  _spec/defs/claudecode/claudecode-egress-topology.puml
end note
@enduml
